	abstract = {This paper presents new evidence on why unemployment insurance ({UI}) {beneÖts} a§ect search behavior and develops a simple method of calculating the welfare gains from {UI} using this evidence. I show that 60 percent of the increase in unemployment durations caused by {UI} {beneÖts} is due to a ìliquidity e§ectî rather than distortions in marginal incentives to search (ìmoral hazardî) by combining two empirical strategies. First, I Önd that increases in {beneÖts} have much larger e§ects on durations for liquidity constrained households. Second, lump-sum severance payments increase durations substantially among constrained households. I derive a formula for the optimal {beneÖt} level that depends only on the reduced-form liquidity and moral hazard elasticities. The formula implies that the optimal {UI} {beneÖt} level exceeds 50 percent of the wage. The ìexact {identiÖcationî} approach to welfare analysis proposed here yields robust optimal policy results because it does not require structural estimation of primitives.},
